"""Rename every bttco-prefixed table, index and constraint to ryozan.

Revision ID: 20261031_ryozan_prefix
Revises: 20261031_baseline
Create Date: 2026-10-31

The app began as a studio called BTTCO, so the baseline names every table
``bttco_*`` and every index and constraint after it. This revision renames
them to ``ryozan``: 61 tables (``bttco_users`` -> ``ryozan_users``), 144
constraints (61 primary keys, 68 foreign keys, 15 unique; renaming a primary
key or unique constraint renames its index with it), the 103 other indexes
(``idx_bttco_users_role`` -> ``idx_ryozan_users_role``) and, on Postgres 18+,
the 496 NOT NULL constraints Postgres 18 records as named catalog objects
(``bttco_users_email_not_null`` -> ``ryozan_users_email_not_null``; Postgres 16
and 17 have no such objects, so ``NOT_NULL_*`` runs only on a server >= 18).
There are no sequences (no serial/identity columns), views, functions,
triggers, rules, policies or standalone types in the schema. Tables without the
prefix (``feedback_samples``, ``viber_*``), indexes/constraints whose names do
not contain ``bttco`` (``ix_sms_messages_status``) and columns
(``bttco_users.bttcoin_balance``, the BTTCoin currency) are left alone.

The statements below are literal and deterministic, not catalog queries.
``scripts/gen_prefix_rename.py`` generated them from the catalog of a Postgres
18 database at ``20261031_baseline`` and re-checks them:

    python scripts/gen_prefix_rename.py <pg18-db-at-baseline> --check alembic/versions/20261031_ryozan_prefix.py

Every statement is a catalog-only rename: no table is rewritten, no index is
rebuilt, no constraint is revalidated, and row data and foreign keys are
untouched (foreign keys reference tables by OID, not by name). Each ``ALTER``
takes an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock on its table until the transaction ends, and
Alembic runs the whole upgrade in one transaction, so the renames land together
or not at all and queries on these tables wait for that commit (well under a
second when nothing holds a conflicting lock; a long-running query or an idle
open transaction on any of these tables makes the migration, and everything
queued behind it, wait for it). The API build that runs after this revision
must use the new names and the build before it the old ones: the container
runs ``alembic upgrade head`` before uvicorn starts, and the previous container
keeps serving (and fails on every renamed table) until the new one is healthy.

Downgrade is the exact inverse, in reverse order.
"""

